#' Simulated longitudinal trial with two binary responses
#'
#' A simulated randomized trial in which two binary responses are recorded at a
#' baseline visit and ten follow-up visits, with intermittent missingness and
#' right-censoring. The data were generated by [simulate_biscal()] from the
#' models of the paper with two baseline covariates; they are synthetic and do
#' not come from any real study. The script is in the `data-raw` directory of
#' the package sources.
#'
#' @format A data frame with one row per subject and recorded visit and the
#'   columns
#'   \describe{
#'     \item{`id`}{Subject identifier (1 to 1000).}
#'     \item{`visit`}{Visit index: 0 (baseline) to 10.}
#'     \item{`trt`}{Treatment: 1 for the treated group, 0 for the control
#'       group.}
#'     \item{`x1`}{Continuous baseline covariate (standard normal).}
#'     \item{`x2`}{Binary baseline covariate.}
#'     \item{`y1`}{First response (0 or 1), measured first at each visit;
#'       `NA` when missing.}
#'     \item{`y2`}{Second response (0 or 1); `NA` when missing.}
#'   }
#'
#' @details Subject \eqn{i} is followed up to visit
#'   \eqn{C_i \sim \mathrm{Binomial}(10, \sigma(1.5 + x_{1i}))}, where
#'   \eqn{\sigma} is the logistic function, as in Section 5.2.3 of the paper;
#'   no visit is recorded after \eqn{C_i}. At follow-up visits the first and
#'   second responses are missing with probabilities
#'   \eqn{\sigma(-1.5 + 0.5 x_{1i})} and \eqn{\sigma(-2 + 0.5 x_{1i})} (about
#'   21% and 14% of the follow-up values), less than in the paper's design.
#'   The true coefficients are stored in `attr(simtrial, "coefs")`; the
#'   treatment mainly affects the first response.
#'
#' @source Simulated with `data-raw/simtrial.R`.
#'
#' @examples
#' head(simtrial)
#' table(simtrial$visit)
#' fit <- biscal(cbind(y1, y2) ~ x1 + x2, data = simtrial, id = "id",
#'               time = "visit", treatment = "trt")
"simtrial"
